### What does this question ask?

The interviewer wants you to strip all whitespace from a string using the Stream API. This tests chars(), filtering by character classification, and the standard filter-map-join idiom for rebuilding strings.

### How do you remove whitespace with streams?

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.util.stream.Collectors;

public class RemoveWhitespace {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String raw = "SAV 2001\tHDFC \n";

        String cleaned = raw.chars()
            .filter(c -> !Character.isWhitespace(c))
            .mapToObj(c -> String.valueOf((char) c))
            .collect(Collectors.joining());

        System.out.println("Raw:     '" + raw.strip() + "'");
        System.out.println("Cleaned: '" + cleaned + "'");
    }
}
```

```text
Raw:     'SAV 2001	HDFC'
Cleaned: 'SAV2001HDFC'
```

### What does Character.isWhitespace cover?

| Character | isWhitespace | Description |
|---|---|---|
| ' ' (space) | true | Regular space |
| '\t' (tab) | true | Horizontal tab |
| '\n' (newline) | true | Line feed |
| '\r' (return) | true | Carriage return |
| '\f' (form feed) | true | Form feed |
| Unicode spaces | true | Non-breaking space, etc. |

This is broader than checking for `c == ' '` and covers all Unicode whitespace categories.

### How is the stream approach different from replaceAll?

```java
// Regex approach: shorter but limited to regex patterns
String regex = raw.replaceAll("\\s+", "");

// Stream approach: accepts any predicate
String stream = raw.chars()
    .filter(c -> !Character.isWhitespace(c))
    .mapToObj(c -> String.valueOf((char) c))
    .collect(Collectors.joining());
```

| Feature | replaceAll | Stream filter |
|---|---|---|
| Brevity | Shorter | More verbose |
| Flexibility | Regex patterns only | Any predicate |
| Readability | Familiar to most | Explicit step-by-step |
| Performance | Compiles regex each call | No regex overhead |

The stream version shines when the filtering rule is more complex than whitespace, such as keeping only digits and uppercase letters.

### How do you keep only specific character types?

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.util.stream.Collectors;

public class KeepAlphanumeric {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String input = "SAV-2001 (HDFC) #Active";

        // Keep only letters and digits
        String alphanumeric = input.chars()
            .filter(c -> Character.isLetterOrDigit(c))
            .mapToObj(c -> String.valueOf((char) c))
            .collect(Collectors.joining());

        System.out.println("Alphanumeric: " + alphanumeric);
    }
}
```

```text
Alphanumeric: SAV2001HDFCActive
```

### What are common mistakes?

Mistake 1: forgetting to map to String before joining.

```java
// WRONG: cannot join an IntStream directly
// raw.chars().filter(...).collect(Collectors.joining());
// IntStream does not have collect(Collector)

// CORRECT: map to String objects first
raw.chars()
    .filter(c -> !Character.isWhitespace(c))
    .mapToObj(c -> String.valueOf((char) c))
    .collect(Collectors.joining());
```

Mistake 2: checking only for space character instead of all whitespace.

```java
// WRONG: misses tabs, newlines, Unicode spaces
.filter(c -> c != ' ')

// CORRECT: covers all whitespace types
.filter(c -> !Character.isWhitespace(c))
```

### What about building the result with StringBuilder instead?

```java
String result = raw.chars()
    .filter(c -> !Character.isWhitespace(c))
    .collect(
        StringBuilder::new,
        (sb, c) -> sb.append((char) c),
        StringBuilder::append)
    .toString();
```

This avoids creating intermediate one-character strings but is more verbose. Both approaches produce the same result.

### What about using codePoints() for Unicode safety?

For strings with characters outside the Basic Multilingual Plane, use `codePoints()` and `Character.toString(int)` instead of casting to char.

```java
String unicodeSafe = raw.codePoints()
    .filter(cp -> !Character.isWhitespace(cp))
    .mapToObj(Character::toString)
    .collect(Collectors.joining());
```

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- "How do you remove only leading and trailing whitespace?" Use `strip()` (Java 11+) or `trim()`.
- "How do you collapse multiple spaces into one?" Use `replaceAll("\\s+", " ")`.
- "How do you remove specific characters?" Change the filter predicate to exclude those characters.
